  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.outlines.OutlineAttachment`. I found no gaps.\n\nThe requirement's obligations are: public `kind` and `span` fields, `#[non_exhaustive]`, `new(kind, span)`, and `Clone` and `Debug` implementations. Spans are located under the cited `attachments.span`, which has its own tests and is not reassessed here.\n\n- **`new`, `kind`, `span`:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` builds an attachment with `OutlineAttachment::new(AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\".into()), span)` inside a custom `OutlineLang`. After loading, it asserts that `attachment.kind` equals `AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\")`. It also asserts that slicing the snapshot text at `attachment.span` gives \"é\". This shows `new` stores both fields and that both are publicly readable. The same test checks the original-snapshot span rule: `1..2` splits a multibyte character and `0..3` runs past the end, and both are rejected.\n- **Struct shape, `#[non_exhaustive]`, constructor signature and trait impls:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` runs `check_declaration` for each documented SDK type, including this one. That helper parses the requirement's `rust,ignore` blocks and compares them with rustdoc output.\n  - It compares the struct declaration and the inherent `new` signature.\n  - It asserts that the set of trait impls equals the declared set, here `Clone` and `Debug`.\n  - It requires `#[non_exhaustive]` on any published struct with public fields.\n  - So removing or altering a field, the constructor, `Clone` or `Debug`, or dropping `non_exhaustive`, would fail this test.\n- **`Clone` and `Debug` behavior:** the implementation is `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` on the struct in `contents.rs`. It has no conditional logic or custom transformation, so the presence check in the public API test is enough.\n\nNo obligations are left untested.",
